{# The last page in the borrow popup: it tells the page that opened it and gets out of the way.
   Rendered by lenny_callback in openlibrary/plugins/upstream/lenny.py (#13688).
   Arguments:
     ok           (bool)      — the loan exists
     message      (str|None)  — what went wrong, for the patron; None when ok
     return_url   (str)       — the Open Library page the patron came from
     read_url     (str|None)  — the node's reader; None when not ok
     due          (str|None)  — the due date as the node reported it, if it gave one
     message_type (str)       — must equal lenny.POPUP_MESSAGE_TYPE

   Not a 303 back to the book, which would be shorter: a redirect cannot close
   the window it is in, and the popup has to close for the patron to end up
   looking at the page they started on. The script below is what closes it.

   The content is rendered rather than hidden-until-needed, because it is what
   the patron reads on the one path with no script to run it: JavaScript off.
   Everywhere else the script below sends this window to `read_url` as soon as
   it has told the opener, because a patron who clicked Borrow asked for a
   book and not for a receipt (#13865).

   Standalone rather than wrapped in the site layout, and the reason is in
   lenny._popup_page: the layout drains the flash message this page is trying
   to hand to the window behind it. So it carries its own doctype and style,
   the way interstitial.html.jinja does for its own layout-less caller.
#}
<!DOCTYPE html>
{# The site layout would have supplied one. Without it the window is titled
   with its URL, which on the no-opener paths is a page the patron reads. #}
<title>{{ _("Borrowed") if ok else _("Borrow") }} — {{ _("Open Library") }}</title>
<style>
  body {
    font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", "Noto Sans", Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
    color: hsl(41, 14%, 21%);
    background: hsl(48, 32%, 94%);
    margin: 0;
    padding: 2em 1.5em;
  }
  .provider-borrow-result h1 {
    font-size: 1.4em;
    margin-top: 0;
  }
  .provider-borrow-result a {
    color: hsl(210, 84%, 31%);
  }
</style>
<div id="provider-borrow-result"
     class="provider-borrow-result"
     data-ok="{{ '1' if ok else '0' }}"
     data-return-url="{{ return_url }}"
     data-read-url="{{ read_url or '' }}"
     data-message-type="{{ message_type }}">
    {% if ok %}
        <h1>{{ _("Borrowed") }}</h1>
        {% if due %}
            <p>{{ _("Your loan is due %(due)s.", due=due) }}</p>
        {% else %}
            <p>{{ _("Your loan has been created.") }}</p>
        {% endif %}
        {% if read_url %}
            <p><a href="{{ read_url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">{{ _("Read it now") }}</a></p>
        {% endif %}
    {% else %}
        <h1>{{ _("Borrow") }}</h1>
        <p>{{ message }}</p>
    {% endif %}
    <p><a class="js-provider-borrow-return" href="{{ return_url }}">{{ _("Back to Open Library") }}</a></p>
</div>
<script>
  (() => {
      const result = document.getElementById('provider-borrow-result');
      if (!result) return;
      const ok = result.dataset.ok === '1';

      // Same-origin by the time we are back on an Open Library path, so this
      // read is allowed -- but a popup the patron reused, or one whose opener
      // navigated away, can still throw or be closed.
      let opener = null;
      try {
          opener = window.opener && !window.opener.closed ? window.opener : null;
      } catch {
          opener = null;
      }

      // Before this window navigates anywhere. An unload partway through loses
      // the message, and the message is the only thing that refreshes the book
      // page: the patron would get their reader and come back to a CTA still
      // offering Borrow.
      if (opener) {
          opener.postMessage({type: result.dataset.messageType, ok}, window.location.origin);
      }

      if (ok) {
          // This window becomes the reader rather than closing on a
          // confirmation nobody stays to read. A navigation, not a
          // `window.open`: the browser's transient activation from the Borrow
          // click expired several screens ago -- the node mails a one-time
          // code and waits for it -- so a popup opened from here would be
          // blocked. A window that is already open can always navigate itself,
          // and this is the same window on the no-opener path too, which is
          // how a blocked popup also ends up in the reader.
          //
          // provider_borrow_popup.js deliberately stops closing this window.
          // The two sides have to agree, or the reader is killed as it loads.
          const read = result.dataset.readUrl;
          if (!read) {
              if (opener) window.close();
              return;
          }
          if (opener) {
              // Opened at 520px, which is a consent screen and not a book.
              // Best effort: a browser that refuses leaves a small reader,
              // and nothing below depends on this having worked.
              try {
                  window.resizeTo(Math.min(screen.availWidth, 1200), Math.min(screen.availHeight, 1000));
              } catch {
                  // Some browsers refuse to resize a window they did not size.
              }
          }
          // `replace`, not `assign`: the URL this page was served from carries
          // a spent authorization code and a `state` that `take_state` deleted
          // on read. Leaving it in this window's history means Back re-sends
          // it and the patron is told their borrow request expired.
          window.location.replace(read);
          return;
      }

      // A failure stays on screen to be read. Its link then acts on the page
      // the patron actually came from, rather than loading a full book page
      // into a 520px window.
      if (!opener) return;
      const back = result.querySelector('.js-provider-borrow-return');
      if (back) {
          back.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
              try {
                  opener.location.assign(back.href);
                  opener.focus();
              } catch {
                  return;  // preventDefault not yet called, so the link still navigates
              }
              event.preventDefault();
              window.close();
          });
      }
  })();
</script>
